{
  "dom_id": "step-q161864",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14156477/assets/539115fd470666cb84e3de4b9b3471db6f04d0af6fd3e33897e5115361fe4e7a.png)\n\nThe function $f(x),$ whose graph is shown above, has a stationary point at $x=-3$ and is continuously differentiable everywhere except at $x=1.$  By considering the points indicated in the diagram, determine which of the following statements **must** be true according to the inverse function theorem.\n\nI. $f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $x=-3$\n\nII. ${f}^{-1}(y)$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $y=-0.87$\n\nIII. The derivative of ${f}^{-1}(y)$ at $y=3$ equals $\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)}$",
